# APML: Natural Language QA Review
# SSI Course Production Pipeline
# Version: 1.1.0 (2025-12-18)
# Status: Design
#
# PURPOSE: Post-production QA review for phrase quality
# Flags phrases for human review based on naturalness standards.
#
# DESIGN DECISION: QA Review, not Blocking Gate
# Based on APML 12 Directions assessment:
# - D01 (Parameterization): Tolerances adjustable, not hardcoded gates
# - D07 (Context-Agnostic): Same pattern reusable for audio QA
# - D08 (Explicitness): Flags with reasons > opaque pass/fail
# - D10 (Observability): See what was flagged and why
#
# TTS costs are marginal (~$1/course), so blocking the pipeline
# to prevent audio generation is not cost-justified.

## ============================================================================
## OVERVIEW
## ============================================================================

NATURAL_LANGUAGE_QA:
  name: "Natural Language QA Review"
  type: "QA WORKFLOW (agent-based)"
  version: "1.1.0"
  integration: "Part of Production API QA workflow"

  description: |
    Post-production review of practice phrases for linguistic quality.
    Agent reviews completed course and FLAGS potential issues for human review.
    Does NOT block the pipeline - TTS costs are marginal (~$1/course).

    Applies DIFFERENT standards based on SYLLABLE COUNT (not word count):
    - Short phrases (< 5 syllables): Must be CORRECT (not wrong, can be clunky)
    - Long phrases (≥ 5 syllables): Must be NATURAL (native speaker would say)

  why_syllables: |
    SYLLABLE COUNT is the true measure of cognitive load for language learners.

    Distinction Theory: Learners parse sound streams through progressive distinction:
    1. Sound vs no sound (most basic boundary)
    2. Louder vs quieter (amplitude changes)
    3. Rapid volume change vs continuous (attack/decay)
    4. Syllable vs "just a blob" (rhythmic segmentation)
    5. Recognizable syllable vs something similar to known language
    6. Distinct syllable variations (male vs female voice)
    7. Significant vs irrelevant changes between speakers

    Syllables are the fundamental perceptual unit - sounds stopping and starting,
    mapped to meanings. Word count is an artifact of writing systems (spaces),
    completely irrelevant for non-Roman scripts (Chinese, Japanese, Thai, Arabic).

    Language-agnostic equivalence:
    - "I want to" = 3 syllables (English)
    - "我想要" wǒ xiǎng yào = 3 syllables (Mandarin)
    - "Quiero" = 2 syllables (Spanish)

    All represent similar cognitive load regardless of how they're written.

  methodology_context: |
    Debut phrases (short, used once during LEGO introduction) serve as
    scaffolding - they build up to the full LEGO. Learners hear them once.
    They can be literal, fragmentary, even slightly clunky - as long as
    they are grammatically correct "as far as they go" and could be
    continued by appending or prepending words.

    Eternal phrases (long, used in spaced repetition) come back again and
    again over months of learning. Learners will encounter these 20+ times.
    These MUST be natural - something a native speaker would actually say.
    Zero tolerance for awkward or unnatural constructions.

  workflow:
    trigger: "After Phase 9 (manifest compilation) - course is usable"
    process:
      1: "Agent reviews all baskets with methodology context"
      2: "Flags potential issues with reasoning"
      3: "Human samples flagged items"
      4: "Bulk dismiss OR investigate further"
      5: "Prune bad phrases if confirmed"
      6: "Re-compile manifest if changes made"

  input:
    - "lego_baskets.json (complete course)"
    - "course_manifest.json (for audio context)"

  output:
    - "qa_phrase_flags.json (flagged issues with reasons)"
    - "Human review queue in dashboard"

## ============================================================================
## PHRASE CLASSIFICATION (SIMPLIFIED)
## ============================================================================

PHRASE_CLASSIFICATION:
  description: |
    Classification is based on SYLLABLE COUNT in the known language.
    This is simpler and more linguistically meaningful than the complex
    runtime classification (eternal_count, debut_max positions).

    Syllable count directly measures cognitive load - the perceptual
    units the learner must parse, regardless of writing system.

  algorithm:
    simple_rule: |
      Count syllables in the KNOWN LANGUAGE phrase:
      - < 5 syllables → SHORT (apply CORRECT standard)
      - ≥ 5 syllables → LONG (apply NATURAL standard)

    components:
      rule: "Phrases with is_component: true"
      standard: "CORRECT (always short by definition)"

  rationale: |
    The learning app's runtime classification (eternal_count, debut_max)
    determines WHICH phrases are shown during debut vs revisit.

    The QA review's syllable-based classification determines
    WHAT QUALITY STANDARD to apply during validation.

    These are complementary, not redundant:
    - Runtime: "Show these 5 phrases on revisit"
    - QA: "Is this phrase natural enough for repeated exposure?"

  examples:
    short_phrases:
      - "I want to" → 3 syllables → CORRECT standard
      - "how to say" → 3 syllables → CORRECT standard
      - "我想" wǒ xiǎng → 2 syllables → CORRECT standard
      - "Quiero" → 2 syllables → CORRECT standard

    long_phrases:
      - "I want to learn how to say" → 7 syllables → NATURAL standard
      - "我想学习怎么说" → 6 syllables → NATURAL standard
      - "Quiero aprender cómo se dice" → 9 syllables → NATURAL standard

## ============================================================================
## VALIDATION STANDARDS
## ============================================================================

VALIDATION_STANDARDS:

  SHORT_PHRASES:
    applies_to: "< 5 syllables in known language"
    standard: "CORRECT"
    description: |
      Short phrases are scaffolding - they build up to the LEGO.
      Learners hear them once during introduction.
      They can be fragments, literal, even slightly clunky.

    rules:
      must:
        - "Grammatically correct AS FAR AS IT GOES"
        - "Word order must be correct"
        - "No agreement errors (gender, number, tense)"
        - "Could be continued by appending/prepending words"
        - "LEGO used correctly in context"

      acceptable:
        - "Can be a fragment (doesn't need to be complete sentence)"
        - "Can be literal/direct translation"
        - "Slightly clunky is OK"

      not_acceptable:
        - "Wrong word order"
        - "Agreement errors"
        - "Syntactically broken"

    tolerance: "Clunky OK, WRONG is not OK"

    examples:
      acceptable:
        - "I want to (3 syl) - fragment, continuable"
        - "how to say (3 syl) - slightly literal, correct"
        - "我想 wǒ xiǎng (2 syl) - fragment"

      not_acceptable:
        - "To want I - wrong word order"
        - "I wants to - agreement error"
        - "How say to - syntactically broken"

  LONG_PHRASES:
    applies_to: "≥ 5 syllables in known language"
    standard: "NATURAL"
    description: |
      Long phrases return in spaced repetition - learners hear them 20+ times.
      They MUST sound natural. Zero tolerance for awkwardness.

    rules:
      must:
        - "Native speaker would actually say this"
        - "Grammatically perfect in BOTH languages"
        - "Semantically equivalent (not just structurally parallel)"
        - "Natural word choice (not dictionary-first-entry syndrome)"
        - "LEGO appears in natural context"

      not_acceptable:
        - "Translationese / overly literal"
        - "Awkward constructions"
        - "Unnatural word choice"
        - "Forced or awkward LEGO placement"

    tolerance: "ZERO - must be perfect"

    examples:
      acceptable:
        - "I want to learn how to say something (9 syl) - natural, complete"
        - "Could you tell me how to say this? (8 syl) - idiomatic question"

      not_acceptable:
        - "I am wanting to make learning of how to say - awkward"
        - "For the Italian, how is the saying? - unnatural structure"
        - "I desire to acquire knowledge of speaking - unnaturally formal"

  COMPONENTS:
    applies_to: "Phrases with is_component: true"
    standard: "CORRECT"
    description: "Individual word components for M-type LEGOs"
    rules:
      - "Must be a valid word in the target language"
      - "Translation must be accurate"
      - "Can be isolated (doesn't need sentence context)"
    tolerance: "High - these are building blocks"
    note: "Components are always short by definition"

## ============================================================================
## VALIDATION PROCESS (SIMPLIFIED)
## ============================================================================

VALIDATION_PROCESS:

  per_basket:
    step_1:
      name: "Count Syllables and Classify"
      action: |
        For each phrase in basket:
        1. Count syllables in KNOWN language phrase
        2. Classify: < 5 syllables → SHORT, ≥ 5 syllables → LONG
        3. Components (is_component: true) → always SHORT

    step_2:
      name: "Apply Standard"
      action: |
        SHORT phrases: Is it grammatically correct? (clunky OK)
        LONG phrases: Would a native speaker say this? (must be natural)

    step_3:
      name: "Flag Concerns"
      action: |
        For any phrase that concerns you:
        - Record phrase (known + target)
        - Record syllable count and classification
        - Record WHY it concerns you
        - Suggest fix if obvious

  agent_mindset: |
    Trust your linguistic judgment.
    Don't overthink - if it sounds wrong, flag it.
    If it sounds clunky but short, that's probably OK.
    If it sounds unnatural and it's long, definitely flag it.

## ============================================================================
## OUTPUT FORMAT
## ============================================================================

OUTPUT_FORMAT:

  qa_flags:
    filename: "qa_phrase_flags.json"
    structure:
      metadata:
        course_code: "string"
        reviewed_at: "ISO timestamp"
        total_phrases: "number"
        syllable_threshold: 5

      summary:
        flagged: "number"
        short_flagged: "number (< 5 syllables)"
        long_flagged: "number (≥ 5 syllables)"

      flags:
        - seed_id: "S0042"
          lego_id: "S0042L01"
          phrase:
            known: "I am wanting to make learning"
            target: "Estoy queriendo hacer aprendizaje"
          syllables: 9
          classification: "LONG"
          severity: "ERROR"
          reason: "Unnatural construction - 'wanting to make learning' is not natural English"
          suggestion: "I want to learn"
          target_suggestion: "Quiero aprender"

      by_classification:
        COMPONENT:
          total: "number"
          passed: "number"
          failed: "number"
        DEBUT:
          total: "number"
          passed: "number"
          failed: "number"
        ETERNAL:
          total: "number"
          passed: "number"
          failed: "number"

  validated_baskets:
    filename: "lego_baskets_validated.json"
    description: "Copy of lego_baskets.json with validation metadata"
    additions_per_phrase:
      - "_classification: COMPONENT | DEBUT | ETERNAL"
      - "_validated: true | false"
      - "_validation_notes: string (if issues)"

## ============================================================================
## QA WORKFLOW ARCHITECTURE
## ============================================================================

QA_WORKFLOW:
  description: |
    This is a POST-PRODUCTION QA workflow, not a pipeline phase.
    It runs AFTER Phase 9 (manifest compilation) when the course is already
    complete and usable. It flags issues for human review but does NOT block.

  trigger: "After Phase 9 completes - course is production-ready"

  integration_options:
    option_1_production_api:
      description: "Part of Production API QA workflow (port 3470)"
      endpoint: "POST /api/production/:courseCode/qa/natural-language"

    option_2_standalone:
      description: "Standalone QA service"
      port: 3471
      server: "services/qa/natural-language-qa.cjs"

  endpoints:
    launch:
      method: "POST /qa/natural-language/launch"
      description: "Start QA review for a completed course"
      parameters:
        courseCode: "string (required)"

    status:
      method: "GET /qa/natural-language/status/:courseCode"
      description: "Check QA review progress"
      response: |
        {
          status: "in_progress | complete",
          progress: { reviewed: N, total: M },
          summary: { flagged: N }
        }

    flags:
      method: "GET /qa/natural-language/flags/:courseCode"
      description: "Get flagged issues for human review"

    dismiss:
      method: "POST /qa/natural-language/dismiss"
      description: "Human dismisses a flag (good judgment)"
      body: |
        { courseCode, flagId, reason: "acceptable" | "will_fix_later" }

    prune:
      method: "POST /qa/natural-language/prune"
      description: "Remove a flagged phrase from the course"
      body: |
        { courseCode, legoId, phraseIndex }

      behavior: |
        SIMPLE APPROACH: Delete reference, leave audio orphaned.

        Steps:
        1. Remove phrase from lego_baskets.json at specified index
        2. Mark manifest as stale (needs recompile)
        3. Log what was pruned (for audit trail)
        4. DO NOT delete audio from Supabase or S3

        Why orphan audio is fine:
        - Storage costs ~$0.02/GB/month (pennies)
        - Same audio may be used by other courses
        - Preserves option to restore phrase later
        - No complex reference counting needed

        Future consideration (not v1):
        - Cross-course phrase flagging
        - Orphan cleanup job (quarterly?)
        - Shared phrase issue tracking

      response: |
        {
          success: true,
          pruned: {
            courseCode: "ita_for_eng",
            legoId: "S0004L01",
            phraseIndex: 7,
            phrase: { known: "...", target: "..." }
          },
          audio_status: "orphaned",  // not deleted
          manifest_status: "stale",  // needs recompile
          note: "Audio preserved in Supabase/S3"
        }

    recompile:
      method: "POST /qa/natural-language/recompile"
      description: "Recompile manifest after pruning phrases"
      body: |
        { courseCode }
      behavior: |
        Triggers Phase 9 manifest compilation for the course.
        Only needed if phrases were pruned.
        Can batch multiple prunes before recompiling.

    orphans:
      method: "GET /qa/audio/orphans"
      description: "List audio samples not referenced by any manifest"
      behavior: |
        Compares Supabase audio_samples table against all course manifests.
        Returns audio that exists but is not referenced anywhere.

        Use cases:
        - Visibility into storage usage
        - Identify pruned phrases' leftover audio
        - Inform quarterly cleanup decisions (if ever needed)

        Note: Orphaned audio is NOT a problem. Storage is cheap.
        This endpoint is for visibility, not action.

      response: |
        {
          count: 42,
          total_size_mb: 12.3,
          oldest: "2025-01-15T...",
          newest: "2025-12-18T...",
          samples: [
            { uuid, text, language, voice_id, created_at, size_kb },
            ...
          ]
        }

      query_example: |
        -- SQL equivalent
        SELECT uuid, text, language, voice_id, created_at
        FROM audio_samples
        WHERE uuid NOT IN (
          SELECT DISTINCT unnest(audio_uuids)
          FROM course_manifests
        );

  non_blocking: |
    This workflow does NOT block the pipeline.
    The course is already usable - QA is refinement, not gatekeeping.

    If issues are found:
    1. Dashboard shows flag count
    2. Human samples flagged items
    3. Bulk dismiss OR investigate further
    4. Prune bad phrases if confirmed
    5. Re-compile manifest if changes made (optional)

## ============================================================================
## AGENT PROMPT TEMPLATE
## ============================================================================

AGENT_PROMPT:
  structure: |
    # Natural Language QA Review

    ## Your Task
    Review practice phrases for linguistic quality.
    Flag anything that concerns you - a human will review your flags.

    ## Course: {courseCode}

    ## SYLLABLE-BASED CLASSIFICATION

    Count syllables in the KNOWN LANGUAGE phrase:
    - < 5 syllables → SHORT phrase → CORRECT standard
    - ≥ 5 syllables → LONG phrase → NATURAL standard

    ### SHORT phrases (< 5 syllables)
    Standard: CORRECT
    - Grammatically valid AS A FRAGMENT
    - Could be continued by adding words
    - Does NOT need to be natural/idiomatic
    - Clunky is OK, WRONG is not OK

    ### LONG phrases (≥ 5 syllables)
    Standard: NATURAL
    - Native speaker would actually say this
    - Grammatically perfect in BOTH languages
    - Not translationese or awkward
    - ZERO TOLERANCE for unnaturalness

    ## Why Syllables?
    Syllables are the perceptual unit learners parse from sound streams.
    Word count is meaningless for non-Roman scripts (Chinese, Japanese, Thai).
    "I want to" (3 syl) ≈ "我想要" (3 syl) ≈ "Quiero" (2 syl)

    ## Process
    1. Read all baskets for the course
    2. For each phrase:
       a. Count syllables in known language
       b. Apply appropriate standard (CORRECT or NATURAL)
       c. If it concerns you, flag it with reasoning
    3. Submit flags

    ## Output Format
    For flagged phrases only:
    ```json
    {
      "legoId": "S0042L01",
      "flags": [
        {
          "phraseIndex": 7,
          "known": "I am wanting to make learning",
          "target": "Estoy queriendo hacer aprendizaje",
          "syllables": 9,
          "classification": "LONG",
          "reason": "Unnatural - 'wanting to make learning' not natural English",
          "suggestion": { "known": "I want to learn", "target": "Quiero aprender" }
        }
      ]
    }
    ```

## ============================================================================
## INTEGRATION WITH PIPELINE
## ============================================================================

PIPELINE_INTEGRATION:
  position: "AFTER Phase 9 - Post-production QA"

  flow: |
    Phase 1 (Translation)
      → Phase 2 (Upchunking)
        → Phase 3 (Baskets)
          → Phase 8 (Audio)
            → Phase 9 (Manifest)
              → ✅ Course is COMPLETE and USABLE
              → Natural Language QA (async, non-blocking)

  key_point: |
    The pipeline completes without waiting for QA.
    QA runs asynchronously and flags issues for human review.
    Course can be used while QA is in progress.

  dashboard_integration:
    - "Show QA status alongside course (not blocking)"
    - "Display error count and issue list"
    - "Provide 'Fix Issues' workflow"
    - "Allow re-validation after fixes"

## ============================================================================
## FUTURE ENHANCEMENTS
## ============================================================================

FUTURE_ENHANCEMENTS:
  auto_fix:
    description: "Automatically fix common issues"
    candidates:
      - "Capitalization normalization"
      - "Punctuation standardization"
      - "Simple word choice improvements"
    implementation: "LLM suggests fix, human approves or agent auto-applies"

  severity_tuning:
    description: "Per-course severity configuration"
    use_case: "Some courses may accept higher tolerance for certain issues"

  language_specific_rules:
    description: "Rules tailored to specific language pairs"
    examples:
      - "Chinese: validate tone marks if included"
      - "Spanish: validate accent marks"
      - "Arabic: validate diacritics"

  syllable_threshold_tuning:
    description: "Test different syllable thresholds"
    purpose: "Optimize classification boundaries per language pair"
    current: "< 5 syllables = SHORT, ≥ 5 syllables = LONG"
    note: "5 seems reasonable as default, but may need adjustment for specific language pairs"

